  "query_preview": "..\n\nRidge of high pressure anchored over the region early this morning \nwill maintain influence heading into Sunday as it slowly vacates to \nthe east. This provides a brief window for deeper layer stability to \nexist within the background of prevailing lower amplitude mid level \nwest-northwest flow. This will support dry conditions with a high \ndegree of insolation potential through the afternoon hours. Emerging \nlow level southerly flow will initiate a modest period of warm air \nadvection through the daylight period. This will bring highs into \nthe middle and upper 80s, but with a more manageable humidity level \nnoted with a dewpoint generally hovering near 60 degrees.\n\nAttention for the evening period turns to behavior of convective \nactivity now organizing over the upper Miss valley within a zone of \ndeeper moist/warm air advection and along one of several mid level \nimpulses embedded within the mean flow. Model guidance offers a \ngeneral consensus for this activity to maintain some form of \nintegrity as translates downstream today, reaching the local area \nduring the evening hours. Higher likelihood for this activity to \narrive in a weakening state as instability really diminishes with \neastward extent, perhaps even falling apart or becoming focused over \na smaller footprint. Forecast will scale precipitation chances down \ninto the chance to lower likely category for the 22z-04z window and \nallow near term trends to dictate further revision. Greater moisture \nadvection occurs within the wake of this wave overnight, initiating \na secondary opportunity for convective expansion along the existing \nfrontal boundary/outflow extending across southern lower MI. While \nthere may be a tendency for greater expansion to build southward \ninto the deeper instability residing south of the state line, the \npotential certainly exists for some clusters of marginally surface \nbased cells to work eastward into mainly areas south of I-96 during \nthe overnight hours. SPC continues to highlight this corridor for an \nisolated risk of damaging winds and hail. In addition, potential for \nsome training of cells along the boundary will offer the possibility \nfor areas of heavier rainfall and a localized flash flooding threat. \n\nForecast for Monday carries strong dependence on behavior of the \novernight activity both locally and upstream. There is currently \nstrong evidence across the model spectrum that the more meaningful \nforced ascent and deeper instability will generally remain to the \nsouth and west during the day. There remains at least a lower \nprobability for mainly southern sections to be vulnerable for \nrenewed diurnal development should more tangible forced ascent \nmaterialize. Latest NBM corroborates this noted trend with a sizable \ndownward revision in forecast precip chances for the Monday period. \nThis casts considerable doubt on prospective severe convective \nchances. There perhaps is greater potential for another round of \nconvective expansion Monday night into Tuesday over the northern \nOhio valley, which could spill into southern lower Mi as bouts of \ngreater moist/warm air advection occur along the oscillating \nboundary and within any passing shortwave. In a nutshell, the early \nweek precip forecast carries very low predictability and likely will \nrequire additional revision. Temperatures during this period will \nremain seasonable for mid August.",
